  "why": "The statement asks for a Hamiltonian path in every connected vertex-transitive graph, and no event delivers that. Each result that proves Hamiltonicity on a restricted class (10p family, 10p, 2pq structural case, moderately dense Cayley graphs, polylog-degree expanders) is a case. Each result on longest-cycle length is a bound on the spanning-path question. That includes the linear bound, because a linear cycle is still not a spanning path. I set aside Nakanishi's odd-order claim as not_credible because several signals hold together: no uptake in 27 months on what would be a landmark, later papers still calling the conjecture open and not citing it, unjustified key steps, and the shrinking text history. Du–Zhou on order 6p is scored variant, because the path statement for 6p was already proved by Kutnar–Šparl in 2007. That leaves only a strengthening to cycles. The digraph papers, the cycle-intersection paper and Knuth's decomposition are variants. Mütze's expository article and the Lean PR are none. On AI: Li–Methuku's model supplied group-theoretic arguments that are in the proof, and Ma–Zhao say GPT-6 exploration led to Theorem 1.2, their main criterion. Both are assisted. Both are human-led and checked by the authors.",
